# DESCRIPTION
# Problem from Functions Modeling Change, Connally et al., 3rd ed.
# WeBWorK problem written by Adam Spiegler, <aspiegler@luc.edu>

## DBsubject(Arithmetic)
## DBchapter(Percents)
## DBsection(Calculations)
## Institution(Loyola University Chicago and Fort Lewis College)
## Author(Adam Spiegler and Paul Pearson)
## Level(2)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Functions Modeling Change')
## TitleText2('Functions Modeling Change')
## TitleText3('Functions Modeling Change')
## AuthorText1('Connally')
## AuthorText2('Connally')
## AuthorText3('Connally')
## EditionText1('3')
## EditionText2('4')
## EditionText3('5')
## Section1(3.1)
## Section2('4.1')
## Section3('4.1')
## Problem1(18)
## Problem2('20')
## Problem3('21')
## KEYWORDS('exponential functions','growth rate','growth factor')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Numeric");

$r     = random(2, 6, 1);
$year  = random(3, 6);
$b     = 1 - $r / 100;
$total = (1 - $b**$year) * 100;

$cmp = Compute("$total")->cmp(tolerance => 0.01, tolType => "absolute");

BEGIN_PGML
An investment decreases by [$r]% per year for [$year] years.  By what total percent does it decrease after the [$year] years?

Overall it decreases by [_]{$cmp}% (round your answer to the nearest 0.01%)

[@  helpLink('numbers')  @]*
END_PGML

$rate_remain = 100 - $r;
$decimal     = $rate_remain / 100;
$factor      = round($decimal**$year * 10000) / 10000;
$almost_rate = $factor * 100;
$total_disp  = round($total * 100) / 100;

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
If an investment decreases by [$r]% each year, we know that only [$rate_remain]% remains at the end of one year.  After 2 years there will be [$rate_remain]% of [$rate_remain]% or [` [$decimal]^2 `] left.  After [$year] years, there will be [` [$decimal]^[$year] \approx [$factor] `] or [$almost_rate]% of the investment left.  Thus the investment decays by [$total_disp]%.

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
